From: tburns@mason1.gmu.edu (T. David Burns)
Date: Fri, 24 Jun 1994 12:39:53 -0400
Subject: [#94-6-125] digital cash and free banking

At  1:42 AM 6/24/94 -0700, Eric Watt Forste wrote:
>[...]
>once frozen, how does one *keep* the monetary base frozen?
>There are monitoring issues here that I'm not sure the market can
>handle. Of course, random fluctuations and market-responsive
>fluctuations can be redressed by market-responsive changes in the
>amount of credit issued by banks, but without the physical commodity
>link, what's to prevent manipulation of the monetary base by the
>biggest banks?  [...]

I presume the monitoring function would fall to a clearinghouse. In
Scotland and other places that implemented something close to true free
banking, the clearinghouse took on most of the legitimate functions that
the central bank now fills. The monetary base would be defined by deposits
at the clearinghouse.

That raises the question of what will keep the clearinghouse honest.
Accounting and audits comprise the answer, though I'm not positive whether
it would be near foolproof. The clearinghouse's books are available to
member banks, so if the clearinghouse tries to pull money out of its hat,
the total base money goes up and everybody knows that something's wrong
(though perhaps not immediately where it went wrong). If a conspiracy
arises among every person who has access to the books (and hence knowledge
of the true base money supply), I suppose it's possible in principle. This
indicates to me that part of the design of the clearinghouse should be that
there are lots of people with access to the books, some of whom are
watchdog types who would be hard to corrupt.

Perhaps I'm trivializing things a bit (I don't claim to be an expert on
this stuff), but I don't think manipulating the money supply would be
easier than ordinary embezzling. Commodity money would simplify things, in
that banks would only need to monitor their own dealings with the
clearinghouse and not the clearinghouse's entire operation. But it seems
doable both in principle and in practice.

Another problem would be how to prevent the clearinghouse and member banks
from *openly* increasing the base money supply. It could be enforcedc by
law, by contract, or as part of the clearinghouse's charter, but these
could all be overcome one way or another. We want a market constraint to
hold this gap closed. I suppose we could hope that a competing
clearinghouse could rise up to challenge the first, providing money that
was more stable. But a competitor would certainly have to offer some kind
of commodity redemption.

Perhaps we should just let them inflate as much as they want. How much
could they get away with without a government monopoly and the cloak of
legitimacy the government provides?

[...]
>regs on barter exchanges are very light. And regular banks certainly
>have much stricter reporting requirements than barter exchanges do.
>
>The regs on commodity depositories (to be carefully distinguished from
>commodities *traders* and *brokers*) are much lighter... nearly
>nonexistent at the federal level.

This is very encouraging.

>[...]stories of gold depositories that
>disappeared in the night with their customers valuta. While this is
