From: doug@OpenMind.com (Doug Cutrell)
Date: Fri, 24 Jun 1994 09:52:40 -0800
Subject: [#94-6-127] SCI: Chaos, Complexity, and Reductionism

Derek writes:

> we certainly would like to say that:
>
> memetics and politics are emergent phenomena
> speciation is an emergent phenomenon
> higher mathematics shows emergent order
> and so on.
>
>Please give me the general objective definition of emergence
>(use Prigogine's covariance <delta-n(r1), delta-n(r2)> if you
>like) that covers these cases.

I agree that we cannot find full satisfaction in understanding the term
"emergence" by pure reduction to objective criteria.  I would suggest that
we try to understand the term "emergence" by examining its utility in our
language.  It seems to me that its utility often lies in specifying
"parsimony of description".  I would suggest that the term "emergence"
refers to a qualitative disjointedness between different domains of
discourse, where certain phenomena we would like to discuss are described
with considerably more parsimony in one domain of discourse than in the
other.

