From: Mark Grant <Mark.Grant@isltd.insignia.com>
Mark Grant said:

>>  This was then merged with several demographic and lifestyle
>> databases. The end result was a very good profile of what a "household"
>> (that was their base unit) did with its money.
>
>But if all you're doing is taking money out of your account and sending
>it to somebody else, what sort of a profile can they create ? If they
>can't link buyer and seller, it seems to me that the most they would be
>able to find out would be your total income and outgoings (which would
>be useless as you could simply withdraw cash and pay it back again to
>upset the figures).

It isn't just the traffic in the account that determines this. It is the
traffic in the account, merged with databases of lifestyle information
and demographic information.

I agree that you could just withdraw and deposit money to mess up their
figures. Knowing dollar amounts, dates that the money was transacted
on, address and employer, someone can usually sit down and identify
rent, utilities, phone, and the general basics without knowing the
other end of the transaction. Then knowing how much of the remainder
is used by check / ATM and the day of the month (namely weekday 
or weekend), combined with lifestyle data can paint a rough image.

Lifestyle data takes where you work (SIC code of employer/address/salary (ie
amount and regularity of deposits)), where 
you live (US Census data can tell if you are in an apartment/house,
are likley to have roommate/significant other, single parent...)
If there are several people from the same company at your banking
institution, your profile can be compared to theirs to get 
company "profile" specifics.
Your ATM transactions can also indicate how far from home you tend
to roam. What time your ATM transactions occur have importance.
There are quite a few "indicators" which are compiled 
from this info.This data is weighted. If you get x hits or more you are likely
to fit into a specific catagory. Admittedly this is a guesstimate
of what a person is like. Most people however tend to be
very cattle-like and predictible.

